pve-meta (0.3.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * API and CLI:
    - A read through an array or a scalar is a 400, like the write.
    - DELETE ?view= of a key that is not there writes nothing and answers
      with the current digest; a packaged-only prefix no longer grows a
      cluster copy of itself over it.
    - Deleting a packaged-only prefix whole removes nothing and says 404.
    - GET /meta/guests without has= reads each guest's digest, not its
      document.
    - `pve-meta ls` lists a file that is neither a document nor a snapshot,
      as unknown/<file>.
    - An array's members are checked against its schema's `items`.
    - A dry run refuses a write above the size cap, as the write does.
    - A prefix file that does not load, or a guest document that does not
      parse, is no longer logged to syslog on every read of it: the
      listing's failure row, the read's parse_error or its 422 already
      say so.
    - `pve-meta set` no longer reads the view back to print "kept N
      note(s)"; a replace keeps the notes it did not carry, as before.
    - A notes block header whose digest holds a multi-byte character no
      longer panics the restore that imports it.
    - `pve-meta get --comments` prints a document that does not parse as
      its raw text, with the parse error on stderr, as the 422's message
      says; before, the read was the 422 whatever the options.
    - `pve-meta set`, `merge` and `delete` refuse an id that is neither a
      vmid nor prefixes/<name> before taking the lock, instead of waiting
      out its 30 s timeout or warning about an undefined value.
  * Lifecycle:
    - The snapshot, delete-snapshot and rollback hooks take the document
      lock, like create and destroy, so a copy or a rollback never
      interleaves with an in-flight write.
    - A forced restore of a backup that carries no block replaces the
      document: the one the guest had, and its snapshot copies, are
      removed.
    - A restore imports the last notes block, the one the backup wrote, and
      strips every block from the notes; a block pasted into them earlier
      no longer wins. The snapshot copies of the document it replaced are
      removed.
    - The hooks take the document lock through one soft-fail helper,
      PVE::Meta::Hooks::locked_warn, shipped in libpve-meta-rs-perl,
      instead of seven copies of it in the patch.
  * Guest files:
    - pve-meta-guest-files is deprecated, managed plane included, and is
      removed in 0.4: pve-meta writes nothing into guests (decision 029,
      superseding 023). A service in a container reads its document over
      the API with a scoped token instead; an operator pushes its own
      files with pct push. The daemon logs a warning when it starts.
  * Editor:
    - A popup (Edit, Add Key, Edit selection as text, New Prefix) stays
      open on a failed write, with what was typed, instead of closing over
      a 400 or a 403.
    - A 409 with a popup open re-reads the document around it: the alert
      says whether the edited value itself changed, naming both values if
      so, and the next OK carries the fresh digest. A 409 in Text mode
      keeps the buffer and diffs it against the file as it is now. New
      Prefix says the name exists.
    - Add refuses a key the document already holds instead of replacing
      its value.
    - The YAML | JSON toggle gives back the exact text it took.
    - A repaired document goes back to the tree without a page reload.
    - A write keeps the row that was selected.
    - The wasm core is installed under its content hash and Monaco under
      its version, so a browser never pairs an upgraded script with a
      cached core.
    - A failed core or Monaco load is retried on the next use; a Monaco
      load that never answers times out after 30 seconds.
    - Hovers work in every text buffer, not only the first panel's; the
      prefixes grid comes back from a failed Reload; the subtree window
      of a read-only document is a view, without OK; the panel closes its
      editors with it; schema squiggles wait for a pause in the typing.
    - A parse error, a removed path, a hover and an enum member in the row
      editor's list are shown as text, not markup. A document that does
      not parse says so above its text.
    - New Prefix writes the tag it checked, trimmed. A number enum opens on
      its value, and an empty number field writes nothing. Append opens on
      the list's item type; Declare Key needs no selected row; a note
      whose key is not set can be removed.
    - Revert in JSON stays reverted, and a YAML-only edit behind JSON
      counts as a change. A read-only caller's Text card has no Apply.
  * Packaging:
    - Depends on pve-ext (>= 0.2.1): the lifecycle manifest is JSON now,
      installed as pve-meta-lifecycle.json, and id-less.
    - The triggers also watch each patched file's .pve-ext-orig name,
      which dpkg writes an upgrade to while the file is diverted: an
      upgrade of libpve-guest-common-perl, qemu-server or pve-container
      re-applies the lifecycle patch on the new file, instead of leaving
      the old patched copy live until a manual pve-ext-patch apply.
    - The regex crate is no longer a dependency.
    - docs/LIFECYCLE.md: the snapshot hooks take the document lock on
      its own, after the guest lock.

 -- Julius Arkenberg <julius@rkenberg.de>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 03:32:34 +0200

pve-meta (0.3.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Managed syncs never collect: a present file the call did not ask for
    stays with its record, since callers sync partial sets. Missing files
    are forgotten by whoever notices. (v0.3.1's same-operator collection
    ate a caller's own files between two syncs; no consumer shipped it.)

