pve-meta (0.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Access is PVE's ACLs and nothing else: VM.Audit reads a guest's document,
    VM.Config.Options writes it, Sys.Modify writes a prefix file. The
    permission files under /etc/pve/meta.d/permissions, their scopes, partial
    reads, per-path write checks, GET /meta/permissions, the permissions/<name>
    documents and the editor's Access column, Add Rule window, Permissions
    grid and service-token wizard are gone. A token that could read a prefix
    through a permission file needs VM.Audit on the guests now; a leftover
    permission file is ignored. GET /meta/access answers { read, write }.
  * A node's schema is an override inside the prefix file: nodes.<node> with
    any of schema, enforce and hidden, each replacing the top-level value
    whole for guests on that node. The per-node directory
    /etc/pve/nodes/<node>/meta.d/prefixes, the nodes/<node>/prefixes/<name>
    ids and routes and the all=1 listing are gone; a file left in that
    directory is ignored and should be folded into the cluster file's
    nodes: map. GET /meta/prefixes?id=<vmid> returns the prefixes reaching
    the guest with selector and override applied, most specific first.
  * GET /meta/version is one token over every document and prefix file; the
    id and detail parameters and the per-document digests are gone.
  * Notes (keys ending in __) follow one rule: a read leaves them out unless
    it asks with comments=1, a replace that did not ask keeps the note of
    every map key it keeps and nothing inside a list, and deleting a key
    deletes its note. A payload that carries a note without asking writes
    it; a view or has= naming a note is an absent path, not a 400.
  * The editor writes each edit at once, as every PVE panel does: a row
    editor's OK is one PUT with the digest, Remove is a DELETE, a 409
    reloads, a 422 names the paths and offers Save anyway. Staged edits,
    Apply and Revert for the tree, the version poll, the Declare Key form
    and the layout-only dialog are gone; the text card keeps Apply, Revert
    and the diff. Declare Key opens schema.properties in the text editor.
  * pve-ext: the manifest id, the .claimed-by marker, the manifest-id
    subcommand, the requires gating of tabs and the iframe page form are
    gone; a leftover marker is ignored.
  * pve-meta-guest-files (renamed since 0.1.x, when it was pve-meta-publish):
    the document key is `guest-files`, not `publish`; the daemon, package,
    prefix file and manifest (now `/etc/pve-meta/.guest-files`) follow the
    new name. A path has no length cap of its own, and the manifest's
    entry name and hash are the daemon's own and otherwise unchecked.
  * The design is docs/DESIGN.md, 150 lines of model, invariants and
    non-goals; the code, its comments and its tests are about half their
    size, with every rule tested once.

 -- Julius Arkenberg <julius@rkenberg.de>  Mon, 21 Sep 2026 17:09:00 +0200

pve-meta (0.1.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Prefix files per node: /etc/pve/nodes/<node>/meta.d/prefixes/<name>.yaml,
    above the cluster's and the packaged one, reaching the guests that node
    currently holds. A guest's set is asked for by vmid -- GET /meta/prefixes
    ?id=<vmid> resolves its node on the server -- and all=1 lists every node's
    files for the Datacenter grid. A node file is a document with the id
    nodes/<node>/prefixes/<name>; writing one is Sys.Modify on /nodes/<node>,
    and only creating one requires the node to still be in the cluster, so a
    departed node's leftovers can be read and removed like any other file. The
    guest tab follows its guest across a migration; the grid gains a Node
    column and the New dialog a location.
  * Comment keys are notes: a read leaves them out unless it asks for them
    with comments=1 (pve-meta get --comments). A replace without them keeps
    the notes of the keys it keeps, so a script that never saw a note cannot
    drop it, and deleting a key takes its note along. The editor asks for
    them and is unchanged. Operators and other consumers stop receiving
    description keys they never wanted.
  * No cluster filesystem, no answer: every store operation refuses with 503
    while /etc/pve is not pmxcfs, instead of reporting an empty store, and an
    I/O error is never read as "absent". An operator acting on a document
    that briefly looked gone cannot delete what it manages.
  * New package pve-meta-publish, optional and not pulled in by pve-meta: a
    per-node daemon that writes views of an LXC guest's own document into
    files inside it, one way, driven by a publish prefix. Relative paths land
    under /etc/pve-meta, absolute paths where they point; yaml, json or raw;
    mode and owner per entry; never a comment key. A hash manifest in the
    guest keeps a file that was edited by hand (local_edits, sync --force)
    and deletes only a file that still matches what it wrote. Write access to
    publish is root inside that guest -- the README says so where the package
    is introduced.
  * sha2 0.11.

 -- Julius Arkenberg <julius@rkenberg.de>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 21:40:00 +0200

pve-meta (0.1.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Backups carry the guest's metadata document, and restores bring it back.
    vzdump appends the document to the archive's copy of the guest's notes as
    one marked block of plain YAML; a restore on a host with pve-meta reads it
    into /etc/pve/meta and strips it before the config lands, on the config
    write that follows the guest's create and on no other. Works for VMs with
    disks, containers, PBS, vma, tar and external providers alike. A host
    without pve-meta restores the block as readable notes text. A document
    above 16 KiB is not carried and the backup log says so. A restore over an
    existing guest now replaces its document from the backup.
  * pve-meta scan-notes reads every such block left in the cluster's guest
    configs into the store and strips it; the package install runs it once.
  * The lifecycle hooks now fire for QEMU guests. qemu-server hands the vmid
    to Perl as a string, which the bindings refused, so create, destroy and
    the snapshot hooks had been warning and doing nothing on every qm
    operation since 0.1.0.
  * Two more managed patches, one file each in qemu-server and pve-container
    (the vzdump assemble hooks), tracked by the ceiling watcher alongside
    libpve-guest-common-perl.

 -- Julius Arkenberg <julius@rkenberg.de>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 15:00:00 +0200

pve-meta (0.1.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Schema checks: type, enum and minimum/maximum are independent, and enum
    membership is by value. A value in the enum no longer skips the type and
    range checks, and the string "1" is no longer the integer 1.
  * A prefix whose schema uses a known keyword with a value the checker could
    not act on (type: interger, enforce: yes, minimum above maximum, an enum
    member of the wrong type) is refused on load and on write. Unknown
    keywords still pass.
  * Registry precedence is by presence: a malformed cluster override shadows
    the packaged file of its name and is listed as the one row for it, and
    GET prefixes/<name> opens that same file. The packaged file no longer
    silently stays in effect while the override is broken.
  * pve-meta ships no live prefix. The traefik example moves from
    /usr/share/pve-meta/prefixes/ to /usr/share/doc/pve-meta/examples/.
    A cluster that relied on the packaged traefik prefix keeps its documents
    and loses the declaration; copy the example into /etc/pve/meta.d/prefixes/
    to have it back.
  * Build: the toolchain is pinned by rust-toolchain.toml; LICENSE at the
    repository root; Dependabot and a RustSec audit workflow.

